# Intent: Named timers on the face with live countdowns for several at once (features.md, core rows). maipai_face_timers_core.h is plain C: the countdown text (4:32, 1:02:03, 0:00 when due), the chip text (the label or the kind word, cut to nine characters, then the countdown; the kind words are English and Spanish), picking what shows (soonest first, ties by id, at most four chips; with more than four, three show by name and the fourth says +N), and the place of each chip (a bottom-anchored grid of up to two rows of two, a lone chip on a row centred). maipai_timers.cc publishes a small read-only copy of the active timers every 200 ms under its own leaf lock (maipai_timers_view); the face reads that copy and never takes the timer lock, because the timer task takes the timer lock and then the display lock and the reverse order would deadlock. The chips are plain text objects on the face, redrawn once a second from the face tick, hidden while a timer rings (its own card shows), kept above the clock face, never clickable, and work with the host off. They are removed with the last timer. Behind CONFIG_MAIPAI_FACE_TIMERS (default y); off, no chips and no timer view are compiled.
# Placement: unit (the timers and the screen are on the robot; they work with the host off)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_timers.{h,cc} of this tree (patch 091); new file hal/maipai_face_timers_core.h
# Check after a flash: start three timers by voice (tea 3 minutes, pasta 10 minutes, laundry 1 hour): three chips along the bottom, soonest first, counting down once a second, each with its name; add a fifth and sixth: three named chips and a +N chip; cancel them all: the chips go; let one ring: the chips hide and the ring card shows; unplug the host and the chips keep counting.
